## Evidence status

Neither legacy assignments nor missing assignments authorize a bank value. Runtime structure, lexical grounding and lexical sign coverage are measured separately. A successful parse is not a certificate of complete meaning. Sparse publication retains the same replay, audit and validation gates as the ongoing build.

## Measured checkpoint scope on 021

The active resource remains `sparse-release-021.db`, SHA256 `91e744cdb4012738d7c9c15929c39cd44211306f75613aeea8e4409708cc6e00`. The existing 021 release receipt supplies its whole-population replay and publication evidence. The new public-entry-point probes supplement that evidence; they do not replace it or certify general language accuracy.

| Channel | Observation | Interpretation and limit |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Sentence structure | All 16 declared root/argument expectations identified, on both sparse and legacy resources | The structural path remains operational; these expectations do not test every semantic field |
| Scoped claims | Negation, possibility and both conditional children satisfy the declared expectations | The indirect-speech complement in “Alice says that Bob left” is instead marked committed on both resources; recorded readiness finding |
| Complete document context | Three two-sentence documents read through intake, resolution, fold and graph; inspected actors and pronoun bindings agree | Six utterances and three documents, not an open-domain coreference score |
| Ordinary lexical projection | 49 content-token occurrences: sparse emits signed values for 1, preserves ambiguity for 47, and has 1 no-row result; legacy emits values for 48 | Emission is availability, not correctness; the old file is not semantic gold |
| Exact lexical identity | Four public `lookup_definition` / `encode_definition` controls succeed with their open domains retained | Dog and cat have distinct selected identities and equal justified assignments, as the design permits |
| Boundary handling | Empty inputs remain empty; null and duplicate IDs are refused | No invented empty-input reading |
| Existing regression suite | 127 tests passed across complete-document intake, claim semantics and lexical-grounding constructions | Regression scope only; the new indirect-speech case is additional evidence |

The runtime does not lack all the content withheld in the ordinary-input sample. Exact dog and cat definitions already carry ALIVE=+1, BODY=+1, SOMETHING=+1 and GOOD=0. Exact chase carries MOVE=+1 and HAPPEN=+1. The unselected ordinary words cannot use those signs. `LexicalGrounding._senses` currently constructs witnessed noun senses, and `GSEEncoder.encode` invokes that contextual selection for nominal tokens. Verb sense grounding therefore needs its own integration through existing structural/source evidence. Guessing a first sense would not satisfy this obligation.

The raw Comparison tree's axis is absent in this sample. The shared `claim_key` consumer explicitly recovers a unique represented modifier as an axis; the raw field alone therefore does not establish a missing comparison capability. The composed-consumer probe checks the actual decision. A type assertion also produces a `missing_predicate` diagnostic for its copula despite retaining the TypeAssertion tree; that diagnostic remains recorded independently of the lexical build.

The foundation census finds 58 scalar chart correspondences: 14 have matching own-coordinate singletons and 44 retain an open own-coordinate domain. There are no opposing singleton signs in this comparison. Most correspondences are still recorded as proposed rather than assignment-approved. This is concentrated specification/teaching work, not permission to import the chart as complete signatures. Other banks remain open unless separately justified.

## Legacy pattern discovery

The read-only inventory confirms 67,111 spelling rows and 124,930 stored bank rows, with 67,045 missing glosses and no explicit zeros. Its frequent exact signature groups include 25,735 SOMETHING-only spellings, 7,656 DO/SOMETHING spellings and 3,829 ALIVE/SOMEONE/SOMETHING spellings. ALIVE and SOMEONE co-occur explicitly on 6,791 spellings; BODY and PART on 1,094. These are locations to investigate causal lexical mechanisms. An absent legacy row supplies no zero, and a same-spelling match supplies no exact native sense identity.

The legacy observations enter none of the staged resolver assertions. Each proposal's actual scope comes from current exact native source witnesses and its stated warrant. Legacy spelling suggestions are retained as discovery provenance, with `SPELLING_ONLY_NOT_SENSE_MATCHED` status.

